**I would now settle on 25 States**, with the important caveat that this is the constitutional minimum only if we do not add the Senate filibuster as an extra constraint.

The 26-State position is not really more self-sufficient. A 26-State coalition has 52 Senators, but that is still far short of the 67 needed to override a presidential veto. So 26 still depends on a friendly President to sign Article IV statehood legislation. But if the coalition can elect a friendly President, it can elect a friendly Vice President on the same ticket. Thus the VP tie-break is not an “outside” actor in the relevant sense; it is part of the coalition’s ordinary electoral strategy.

A 25-State coalition can be chosen as, for example, the 25 most populous States. That gives it:

- 50 Senators;
- more than 270 electoral votes;
- a House majority;
- the ability to elect a friendly President and Vice President within the 51-month window.

With a friendly VP, the Senate can break a 50–50 tie and pass ordinary Article IV statehood/admission legislation. The friendly President can sign it. The coalition States’ legislatures can consent to subdivision. Then the coalition can split its own friendly States to create enough new friendly States. Starting with 25 friendly and 25 opposed, it needs 75 friendly out of 100 total, since \(75/100 = 3/4\). So it can add 50 new friendly States by splitting its own consenting States. After that, it controls enough States for both Article V proposal and ratification.

Fewer than 25 is impossible. With 24 States, the coalition has at most 48 Senators; even a friendly VP cannot break a 48–52 deficit. It also falls short of the direct Article V convention threshold of 34 States. So 24 cannot even start the Article IV route.

The 26 answer only becomes correct if one artificially forbids the coalition from electing a cooperative Vice President, but that is inconsistent because 26 also needs a cooperative President. And if we did count the Senate filibuster, then neither 25 nor 26 would suffice; the threshold would be higher. Under the problem’s stated standard—strict constitutional text and uncontroversial Supreme Court precedent—the minimum is **25 States**.

